{"author":{"id":"b409816c5cd5ab140d1d52eb33aace35de44952bb794b19806fd57f6ecb1ace9"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1793986114,"nonce":"656Gja1phEvAHULho69jXyhoFrM=","title":"List copies, slices and joins allocate their buffer twice","message":"A fresh list starts with a four-slot buffer, so a slice, copy or concatenation of more than four elements reallocated it: the initial buffer, a second one, a copy and a free, plus a call to memset. Python micro, 5 to 8 element lists (ns per operation, Mac, zypy against PyPy): a[3:] 92 against 6, a[:] 88 against 5, a + b 96 against 10. Fixed for the library's slice, copy and concatenation by sizing the buffer once: a[3:] 57, a[:] 57, a + b 63; slices of up to four elements are unchanged. Still open here: list and set comprehensions, repeat and the other empty-then-reserve sites, and the second allocation for the list header (a one-block small list); the rest of the 90 ns is two allocator round trips and the library call.","files":null}]}